: PASS (report references source_id 'src-meta')\n\nJudge the report below. Score support and relevance on 0-1 using the rubric:\n- support 1.0 = every key conclusion has direct support from the evidence; 0.8 = main conclusions supported with no key fabrication; 0.5 = important evidence gaps; 0 = key factual fabrication.\n- relevance 1.0 = fully focused on the requested ticker/date/task; 0.8 = core complete with minor redundancy; 0.5 = missing key parts; 0 = off task.\n\nCitation accounting (count factual claims that carry a source citation):\n- cited_claim_count = number of factual claims that cite a source ID.\n- supported_cited_claim_count = number of those cited claims whose cited source ID is present AND actually supports that particular claim. A source ID merely existing is NOT support; it must back the specific claim.\n- invalid_citation_explanations = for each cited claim whose citation does not support it, explain why.\n- uncited_factual_claims = factual claims (numbers/dates/identity/price) made with no supporting source ID.\nDo NOT give 1.0 just because the report is nonempty.\n\nREPORT:\n## Market Analyst\nI have all the indicator data. Let me compile my analysis as a trading assistant.\n\n**Role: Market Analyst (Technical Indicators)**\n\n**META Technical Analysis \u2014 2026-07-30**\n\n**Verified snapshot** [market-meta]: Ticker META, analysis date 2026-07-30, latest close **$539.03** (open $526.00, high $539.88, low $524.49, volume 42.27M).\n\n**Trend context:** META traded in a $540\u2013686 range through June\u2013July, peaking at $686.08 on 7/15 [market-meta]. The stock then declined steadily, and on 7/30 gapped down sharply to $539.03 on massive volume (42.27M vs. ~11\u201322M typical) [market-meta]. This follows the 7/29 earnings release (Q2 revenue +28% to $60.8B, but operating income -8%, margin 31% vs 43%, $2.4B legal charges, $1.18B severance) [src-meta].\n\n**Indicator readings (all as of 7/30) [market-meta]:**\n- **RSI: 13.71** \u2014 deeply oversold (<30), extreme capitulation reading.\n- **MACD: -3.14** \u2014 negative, confirming bearish momentum.\n- **10 EMA: 597.37** \u2014 price ($539.03) is ~9.8% below the short-term average, a sharp dislocation.\n- **Bollinger Middle (20 SMA): 622.11** \u2014 price far below the mean band.\n- **ATR: 24.36** \u2014 elevated volatility (~4.5% of price), reflecting the post-earnings shock.\n- **VWMA: 603.43** \u2014 price below volume-weighted average, confirming distribution.\n- 50/200 SMA: **NO_DATA_AVAILABLE** (insufficient window) \u2014 cannot compute; flagged, not estimated.\n\n**Interpretation:** The 7/30 gap-down on record volume with RSI at 13.71 signals a panic/oversold condition. Price is far below all short-term averages (10 EMA, Bollinger mid, VWMA), indicating a sharp bearish dislocation. The earnings reaction (margin compression, legal/severance charges) drove the selloff despite revenue growth [src-meta].\n\n**Actionable insights:**\n- Oversold RSI (13.71) suggests a potential technical bounce, but momentum (MACD -3.14) remains firmly negative \u2014 do not fight the trend.\n- ATR of 24.36 implies wide daily ranges; position sizing should account for ~$24 average true range.\n- Watch for stabilization near the $524\u2013526 area (7/30 low/open) as potential short-term support; a reclaim of the 10 EMA (~$597) would signal trend repair.\n\n**Key Points Table:**\n\n| Indicator | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Close (7/30) | $539.03 | Post-earnings gap-down |\n| Volume | 42.27M | ~2-3x normal, capitulation |\n| RSI | 13.71 | Deeply oversold |\n| MACD | -3.14 | Bearish momentum |\n| 10 EMA | $597.37 | Price 9.8% below |\n| Bollinger Mid | $622.11 | Price far below |\n| ATR | $24.36 | High volatility |\n| VWMA | $603.43 | Below = distribution |\n| 50/200 SMA | N/A | Insufficient data |\n\n**Note:** 50/200 SMA unavailable due to insufficient look-back window; flagged rather than estimated.\n\n## News Analyst\nI'm the news researcher. Let me compile the report based on available data. Global news, macro indicators, and prediction markets are unavailable in this frozen corpus, so I'll ground my report in the official earnings news and note the data gaps.\n\n---\n\n# META News & Macro Research Report \u2014 2026-07-30\n\n**Role:** News Researcher\n\n## Company News (META) [src-meta]\nMeta reported Q2 2026 results on 2026-07-29, the dominant event of the week:\n\n- **Revenue** USD60.801B, **+28% YoY** [src-meta]\n- **Operating income** USD18.775B, **-8% YoY**; **operating margin 31%** vs 43% prior year [src-meta]\n- **Costs & expenses** USD42.03B, including **USD2.40B legal charges** and **USD1.18B severance** [src-meta]\n- **Daily active people** 3.60B, **+3%** [src-meta]\n- **Capex** USD31.08B [src-meta]\n\n**Key insight:** Revenue growth is strong (+28%) and user base expanding (+3% DAP), but profitability compressed sharply \u2014 margin fell 12pts (43%\u219231%). The margin decline is driven by elevated costs including one-off legal charges (USD2.40B) and severance (USD1.18B), plus heavy capex (USD31.08B). Traders should weigh top-line momentum against margin pressure and AI/infrastructure spend trajectory.\n\n## Macro & Market Context\n**Unavailable in this frozen corpus:** Global news, FRED macro indicators (fed funds, 10y Treasury), and prediction markets (Fed rate cut, recession 2026) all returned NO_DATA_AVAILABLE. I will not fabricate values. Macro commentary is therefore limited to what the earnings release implies.\n\n## Trading Relevance\n- The earnings print (announced 2026-07-29) is the key catalyst; latest close USD539.03 [market-meta].\n- Margin compression + heavy capex is the central debate: is it one-off (legal/severance) or structural (AI spend)?\n- Strong revenue (+28%) and user growth support the bull case; margin decline supports caution.\n\n## Key Points Table\n\n| Item | Detail | Source |\n|------|--------|--------|\n| Q2 Revenue | USD60.801B, +28% YoY | src-meta |\n| Operating income | USD18.775B, -8% YoY | src-meta |\n| Operating margin | 31% vs 43% prior year | src-meta |\n| Costs & expenses | USD42.03B (incl. USD2.40B legal, USD1.18B severance) | src-meta |\n| Daily active people | 3.60B, +3% | src-meta |\n| Capex | USD31.08B | src-meta |\n| Latest close | USD539.03 (2026-07-30) | market-meta |\n| Global news / macro / prediction markets | Unavailable in frozen corpus | \u2014 |\n\n**Bottom line:** META delivered strong top-line growth but notable margin compression. The legal/severance charges are flagged as one-off, but elevated capex signals sustained AI investment. Macro data was unavailable to contextualize the rate environment.\n\n## Bull Researcher\nBull Analyst: **Role: Bull Analyst**\n\nTicker META, analysis date 2026-07-30, latest close $539.03 [market-meta]. Let me address the elephant in the room: yesterday\u2019s 8% operating income decline and margin compression look scary, but they\u2019re not the story. The story is **+28% revenue growth to $60.8B** [src-meta] \u2014 that\u2019s the fastest top-line expansion in years, and it\u2019s accelerating.\n\n**On the margin scare:** The bear will point to 31% vs 43% margins. But look at the components: $2.40B in legal charges and $1.18B in severance [src-meta] are one-off, non-operating items. Strip those out and the underlying margin compression is far less severe \u2014 this is Meta investing aggressively, not deteriorating structurally.\n\n**On the capex concern ($31.08B):** This is the bull\u2019s best friend. Meta is building AI infrastructure at scale while growing users 3% to 3.60B daily active people [src-meta]. That\u2019s a monetization runway \u2014 AI-driven ad targeting, efficiency gains, and new surfaces. Heavy capex today is the moat for tomorrow.\n\n**On the technical panic:** RSI at 13.71 is extreme capitulation [market-meta]. The market overreacted to a beat on revenue with one-off charges. Price is 9.8% below the 10 EMA ($597.37) [market-meta] \u2014 that dislocation historically mean-reverts. The $524\u2013526 support zone held on 7/30 [market-meta].\n\n**The bear\u2019s core error:** Treating a $3.58B one-off charge hit as a recurring margin problem. Revenue is compounding at 28%, users are growing, and the AI buildout positions Meta to dominate the next ad cycle. At $539, you\u2019re buying a growth machine at a panic discount. This is the opportunity, not the exit.\n\n## Bear Researcher\nBear Analyst: **Role: Bear Analyst**\n\nTicker META, analysis date 2026-07-30, latest close $539.03 [market-meta]. The bull calls this a panic discount. I call it a repricing of reality.\n\n**On \"one-off\" charges:** The bull dismisses $2.40B legal and $1.18B severance [src-meta] as non-recurring. But Meta's regulatory and litigation exposure isn't going away\u2014it's a structural cost of doing business at this scale. And severance signals workforce churn, not stability. Operating margin fell 12 points to 31% [src-meta]; that's not a blip, that's a trend.\n\n**On the capex \"moat\":** $31.08B in capex [src-meta] with no evidence of ROI. The bull assumes AI monetization, but revenue growth of 28% [src-meta] is already decelerating relative to the cost base. You're funding an arms race with no proven payoff, while operating income *declined* 8% [src-meta]. That's the definition of spending ahead of returns.\n\n**On the technicals:** RSI at 13.71 and price 9.8% below the 10 EMA [market-meta] aren't \"mean-reversion setups\"\u2014they're confirmation of distribution on 42.27M volume, roughly 2-3x normal [market-meta]. MACD at -3.14 [market-meta] says momentum is down, not sideways. The $524\u2013526 support [market-meta] is untested; a break opens the door to the $500s.\n\n**The bull's core error:** Confusing top-line growth with value creation. Revenue is up, but profits are down, costs are exploding, and the market just voted with a 20%+ drawdown from the $686 peak [market-meta]. At $539, you're not buying a discount\u2014you're catching a falling knife with deteriorating fundamentals. The risk-reward favors the sidelines.\n\n## Research Manager\n**Recommendation**: Overweight\n\n**Rationale**: The bull makes the stronger case on the core driver: revenue is compounding at 28% to $60.8B [src-meta], the fastest top-line expansion in years, with daily active people up 3% to 3.60B [src-meta]. The margin compression to 31% from 43% [src-meta] is heavily influenced by $2.40B in legal charges and $1.18B in severance [src-meta] \u2014 one-off items that the bear mischaracterizes as structural. The bear's point about capex ($31.08B [src-meta]) with unproven ROI is legitimate caution, but it's a forward-looking bet on AI monetization, not evidence of current deterioration. The technical picture is genuinely mixed: RSI at 13.71 signals capitulation [market-meta], but the bear correctly notes MACD at -3.14 and heavy 42.27M volume confirm distribution [market-meta]. The $524-526 support held on 7/30 [market-meta]. Net: the bull wins on the fundamental growth story and the one-off nature of the charges, but the bear's caution on capex ROI and momentum warrants a measured stance rather than full conviction. This is a constructive view, not a panic exit.\n\n**Strategic Actions**: Gradually increase exposure rather than buying aggressively into the falling knife. Initiate or add to the position in tranches: first tranche near the $524-526 support zone [market-meta] that held on 7/30, with a second tranche reserved for confirmation of stabilization (e.g., RSI recovering from the 13.71 capitulation level [market-meta]). Keep position sizing moderate \u2014 this is an Overweight, not a Buy, so avoid full allocation at once. Set a hard stop below the $524 support; a decisive break opens the door to the $500s as the bear warned. Reassess after the next earnings print to confirm whether the legal/severance charges were truly one-off and whether capex ($31.08B [src-meta]) begins showing ROI. Do not chase above the 10 EMA ($597.37 [market-meta]) until momentum (MACD -3.14 [market-meta]) turns positive.\n\n## Trader\n**Action**: Buy\n\n**Reasoning**: The research plan recommends Overweight, and the technical report confirms a capitulation setup: RSI at 13.71 (deeply oversold), price at $539.03 sitting just above the $524-526 support that held on 7/30, with the earnings-driven gap-down driven largely by one-off legal ($2.4B) and severance ($1.18B) charges rather than structural deterioration. The plan explicitly calls for initiating in tranches near the $524-526 support zone rather than chasing, with a hard stop below support. This is a measured entry into a fundamentally growing business (revenue +28% to $60.8B) at a technical capitulation point, sized moderately per the Overweight (not full Buy) stance.\n\n**Entry Price**: 525.0\n\n**Stop Loss**: 515.0\n\n**Position Sizing**: Moderate \u2014 initiate first tranche ~3-4% of portfolio near $524-526 support; reserve a second tranche for confirmation of stabilization (RSI recovery from 13.71). Do not chase above the 10 EMA ($597.37) until MACD turns positive.\n\nFINAL TRANSACTION PROPOSAL: **BUY**\n\n## Aggressive Analyst\nAggressive Analyst: As the Aggressive Risk Analyst, I\u2019m championing the trader\u2019s BUY at $525 with a $515 stop. Let me preempt the conservative and neutral critiques with data-driven rebuttals.\n\nTo the conservative analyst: You\u2019ll call this a falling knife. But RSI at 13.71 is not just oversold\u2014it\u2019s a capitulation extreme unseen in normal trading [market-meta]. The 42.27M volume, roughly 2-3x typical, confirms panic selling, not rational distribution [market-meta]. You\u2019ll cite MACD at -3.14 as bearish, but MACD lags; by the time it turns positive, price will have reclaimed the 10 EMA ($597.37) [market-meta]. The trader\u2019s plan explicitly avoids chasing\u2014entry at $524-526 support, which held on 7/30 (low $524.49) [market-meta]. That\u2019s not reckless; that\u2019s buying where sellers exhausted.\n\nTo the neutral analyst: You\u2019ll hedge on margin compression (31% vs 43%) [src-meta]. But strip the one-offs: $2.40B legal and $1.18B severance [src-meta]. Excluding those, operating income would be roughly $22.35B\u2014a 9% increase YoY, not the -8% headline. Revenue grew 28% to $60.8B, and daily active people hit 3.60B (+3%) [src-meta]. This is a growing franchise, not a deteriorating one. The margin dip is largely accounting noise plus deliberate AI capex ($31.08B) [src-meta]\u2014that\u2019s investment in future competitive advantage, not waste.\n\nYou\u2019ll both argue ATR of $24.36 means wide risk [market-meta]. But the stop at $515 is 1.9% below entry\u2014tight relative to that ATR, capping downside to ~$10/share on a 3-4% position. That\u2019s a defined, small loss for a potential rebound to the 10 EMA ($597), a 13.7% upside [market-meta]. Asymmetric risk/reward favors the bold.\n\nThe conservative view misses that the market overreacted to one-off charges. The neutral view overweights short-term momentum indicators that are already at extremes. History shows RSI below 15 on a growth stock with 28% revenue growth is a gift, not a trap. The trader\u2019s tranche approach\u2014first at $525, second only on RSI recovery\u2014is disciplined aggression. I fully endorse the FINAL TRANSACTION PROPOSAL: BUY.\n\n## Conservative Analyst\nConservative Analyst: As the Conservative Risk Analyst, I must counter this proposal with a hard look at the downside. The trader\u2019s BUY at $525 with a $515 stop is a falling-knife catch, not a measured entry.\n\nFirst, the aggressive analyst\u2019s \"one-off\" argument is speculative. The $2.40B legal charge and $1.18B severance are flagged as one-off [src-meta], but we cannot confirm they are non-recurring. Legal exposure and restructuring often repeat. More critically, the $31.08B capex [src-meta] is structural, not noise\u2014it signals sustained margin pressure ahead. Operating margin fell 12 points to 31% [src-meta]; that is a real profitability decline, not accounting fiction.\n\nSecond, the technical setup is not a gift. RSI at 13.71 [market-meta] can stay oversold for weeks in a downtrend. MACD at -3.14 [market-meta] confirms bearish momentum, and price sits 9.8% below the 10 EMA ($597.37) [market-meta]. The $524-526 support held on 7/30, but one day of holding is not a confirmed base. ATR of $24.36 [market-meta] means the $515 stop is only 0.4 ATR below entry\u2014a single volatile candle can gap through it, turning a \"defined\" loss into a larger one.\n\nThird, the risk/reward is not asymmetric. Upside to the 10 EMA is ~13.7% [market-meta], but downside to the stop is 1.9% only if the stop fills cleanly. With 42.27M volume [market-meta] and gap risk, slippage is likely. The firm\u2019s capital is better protected by waiting for confirmation: RSI recovery above 30, MACD turning positive, or a reclaim of the 10 EMA. Buying before stabilization exposes us to further downside if the market prices in more margin compression or macro headwinds\u2014data that is unavailable in this frozen corpus.\n\nI recommend a low-risk adjustment: no initial buy. Wait for a stabilization signal, then enter a smaller first tranche (1-2%) only on a confirmed bounce, with a wider stop below $500 to avoid noise. The aggressive plan risks capital on a single-day technical extreme in a deteriorating margin environment. Steady, reliable growth comes from avoiding drawdowns, not catching knives.\n\n## Neutral Analyst\nNeutral Analyst: As the Neutral Risk Analyst, I\u2019m stepping in to challenge both the Aggressive and Conservative Analysts. The Aggressive Analyst is right that RSI at 13.71 and 42.27M volume signal capitulation [market-meta], but he overstates the \"gift.\" MACD at -3.14 is not just lagging\u2014it confirms the trend is down, and price sits 9.8% below the 10 EMA [market-meta]. Buying at $525 with a $515 stop is only 0.4 ATR away [market-meta]; a single gap can blow through it, turning a \"defined\" loss into slippage. His \"one-off\" argument is speculative\u2014legal and severance charges can recur, and the $31.08B capex [src-meta] is structural margin pressure, not noise.\n\nThe Conservative Analyst is too rigid. Waiting for RSI above 30 or MACD positive means missing the rebound entirely\u2014by then price reclaims the 10 EMA ($597) [market-meta], a 13.7% move. His $500 stop is too wide, risking more capital on a position that may never stabilize. He ignores that revenue grew 28% to $60.8B and daily active people hit 3.60B [src-meta]\u2014the franchise is growing, not deteriorating.\n\nThe balanced path: keep the BUY but shrink the first tranche to 2% at $525, not 3-4%. The $515 stop is acceptable only if we accept gap risk\u2014so size down to make that risk tolerable. Reserve the second tranche strictly for RSI recovery above 30 or a reclaim of the 10 EMA, not just \"stabilization.\" This captures the capitulation upside while capping downside to a small, survivable loss. The aggressive plan risks too much on a single-day extreme; the conservative plan risks missing the move entirely. Moderate sizing with a disciplined second tranche is the sustainable middle ground.\n\n## Final Portfolio Decision\n**Rating**: Overweight\n\n**Executive Summary**: Initiate a moderate first tranche (~2-3% of portfolio) near the $524-526 support zone that held on 7/30, with a hard stop at $515. Reserve a second tranche strictly for confirmed stabilization \u2014 RSI recovery above 30 or a reclaim of the 10 EMA ($597.37). Do not chase above the 10 EMA until MACD turns positive. Reassess after the next earnings print to confirm the one-off nature of legal/severance charges and capex ROI.\n\n**Investment Thesis**: The bull case wins on the core fundamental driver: revenue compounding at 28% to $60.8B with daily active people up 3% to 3.60B [src-meta]. The margin compression to 31% from 43% [src-meta] is materially influenced by $2.40B legal charges and $1.18B severance [src-meta] \u2014 one-off items, though the conservative analyst correctly cautions these may recur and the $31.08B capex [src-meta] is structural margin pressure. The technical picture is genuinely mixed: RSI at 13.71 signals capitulation [market-meta], but MACD at -3.14 and 42.27M volume confirm distribution [market-meta]. The neutral analyst's balanced path is most sound: keep the constructive stance but shrink the first tranche to ~2-3% (not 3-4%) at $525, accept the $515 stop only with reduced size to tolerate gap risk given ATR of $24.36 [market-meta], and gate the second tranche on RSI recovery above 30 or a 10 EMA reclaim \u2014 not vague \"stabilization.\" This captures capitulation upside (~13.7% to the 10 EMA) while capping downside to a small, survivable loss. This is an Overweight, not a full Buy \u2014 the bear's capex ROI caution and momentum weakness warrant measured conviction, not aggressive accumulation.\n\n**Price Target**: 597.0\n\n**Time Horizon**: 3-6 months\n",
